nodes.body:
I don't have resources (i.e. Windows nodes) to debug the issue, but `MkdirAll` already checks if the target exists and returns no error, even on Windows: https://cs.opensource.google/go/go/+/refs/tags/go1.20.7:src/os/path.go;l=18-26

It seems that Windows has a different `Mount` semantics, it creates the parent dir (`\var\lib\kubelet\plugins\kubernetes.io\csi\csi.vsphere.vmware.com\XXXXX\`) and then creates a `global` link there, not a directory. There are traces of this behavior in vSphere in-tree volume code:
https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/0a37f09c32bca1c7b1217d293f9bec63c4af6de1/pkg/volume/vsphere_volume/vsphere_volume.go#L240-L249

CSI spec [says](https://github.com/container-storage-interface/spec/blob/98031ee88670956fd20b2333250ac6e03dc85222/csi.proto#L1320C1-L1327) that:

>   // The path to which the volume MAY be staged. It MUST be an
  // absolute path in the root filesystem of the process serving this
  // request, and MUST be a directory. The CO SHALL ensure that there
  // is only one `staging_target_path` per volume. The CO SHALL ensure
  // that the path is directory and that the process serving the
  // request has `read` and `write` permission to that directory. The
  // CO SHALL be responsible for creating the directory if it does not
  // exist.

It IMHO does not allow the CSI driver to replace the directory with a link, at least not in NodeStage.  Just reading the spec with almost no Windows knowledge, I'd expect that drivers put a link to the device root dir into the `global` directory, e.g. as `\var\lib\kubelet\plugins\kubernetes.io\csi\csi.vsphere.vmware.com\XXXXX\global\link`. What other CSI drivers do on Windows? Shall we rephrase the spec for Windows and then fix kubelet?

NodePublish target dir is a different story, because it's created by the driver and Kubernetes should create only the parent dir:

>   // The path to which the volume will be published. It MUST be an
  // absolute path in the root filesystem of the process serving this
  // request. The CO SHALL ensure uniqueness of target_path per volume.
  // The CO SHALL ensure that the parent directory of this path exists
  // and that the process serving the request has `read` and `write`
  // permissions to that parent directory.
  // For volumes with an access type of block, the SP SHALL place the
  // block device at target_path.
  // For volumes with an access type of mount, the SP SHALL place the
  // mounted directory at target_path.
  // Creation of target_path is the responsibility of the SP.

Here it's pretty clear, kubelet must `MkdirAll` the parent dir and it already works the same on Linux and on Windows, i.e. does nothing when the directory already exists. The driver should "place the mounted directory" there, so if "mount" means "link" on Windows, then kubelet is IMO fine with that.
